.TH "OXTB" "1" "" "" ""
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el       .ds Aq '
.nh
.ad l
.SH "NAME"
.sp
oxtb \- performs semiempirical quantummechanical calculations
(o\-xtb 0.4.0, implementing the xTB 6.7.1 Hamiltonian and the
\f(CRxcontrol(7)\fP input of xTB 6.0 and newer)
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
\fBoxtb\fP [\fIOPTIONS\fP] \fIFILE\fP [\fIOPTIONS\fP]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
The \f(CRoxtb(1)\fP program performs semiempirical quantummechanical calculations.
The underlying effective Hamiltonian is derived from density functional
tight binding (DFTB). This implementation of the xTB Hamiltonian is currently
compatible with the zeroth, first and second level parametrisation for
geometries, frequencies and non\-covalent interactions (GFN)
as well as with the ionisation potential and
electron affinity (IPEA) parametrisation of the GFN1 Hamiltonian.
The generalized born (GB) model with solvent accessable surface area (SASA)
is also available in this version.
Ground state calculations for the simplified Tamm\-Dancoff approximation (sTDA)
with the vTB model are currently not implemented.
.SS "GEOMETRY INPUT"
.sp
The wide variety of input formats for the geometry are supported by using
the mctc\-lib. Supported formats are:
.IP \(bu 2
Xmol/xyz files (xyz, log)
.IP \(bu 2
Turbomole's coord, riper's periodic coord (tmol, coord)
.IP \(bu 2
DFTB+ genFormat geometry inputs as cluster, supercell or fractional (gen)
.IP \(bu 2
VASP's POSCAR/CONTCAR input files (vasp, poscar, contcar)
.IP \(bu 2
Protein Database files, only single files (pdb)
.IP \(bu 2
Connection table files, molfile (mol) and structure data format (sdf)
.IP \(bu 2
Gaussian's external program input (ein)
.IP \(bu 2
JSON input with \f(CRqcschema_molecule\fP or \f(CRqcschema_input\fP structure (json)
.IP \(bu 2
FHI\-AIMS' input files (geometry.in)
.IP \(bu 2
Q\-Chem molecule block inputs (qchem)
.sp
For a full list visit: https://grimme\-lab.github.io/mctc\-lib/page/index.html
.sp
\f(CRoxtb(1)\fP reads additionally \f(CR.CHRG\fP and \f(CR.UHF\fP files if present.
.SH "INPUT SOURCES"
.sp
\f(CRoxtb(1)\fP gets its information from different sources. The one with highest
priority is the commandline with all allowed flags and arguments described
below. The secondary source is the \f(CRxcontrol(7)\fP system, which can in principle
use as many input files as wished. The \f(CRxcontrol(7)\fP system is the successor of
the set\-block as present in version 5.8.2 and earlier. This implementation of
\f(CRoxtb(1)\fP reads the \f(CRxcontrol(7)\fP from two of three possible sources, the local
xcontrol file or the \fIFILE\fP used to specify the geometry and the global
configuration file found in the \f(CRXTBPATH\fP.
.sp
The geometry comes from exactly one positional \fIFILE\fP. A second positional
file, and a positional argument that is neither an option nor an existing
file, abort the run: they used to be warned about and dropped, so a command
line the program did not honour still produced a result (a forgotten level
keyword, for example, left a frequency run on the unrelaxed geometry).
.SH "OPTIONS"
.sp
\fB\-c, \-\-chrg, \-\-charge\fP \fIINT\fP
.RS 4
specify molecular charge as \fIINT\fP, overrides \f(CR.CHRG\fP file and \f(CRxcontrol\fP
option. For an \f(CRoniom\fP calculation the form \fIINT:INT\fP gives the charges of
the \fIinner region:outer region\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-u, \-\-uhf\fP \fIINT\fP
.RS 4
specify number of unpaired electrons as \fIINT\fP, overrides \f(CR.UHF\fP file and
\f(CRxcontrol\fP option (default: 0 (singlet) for even electron number, 1
(doublet) for odd electron number)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-gfn, \-\-gfn0, \-\-gfn1, \-\-gfn2\fP [\fIINT\fP]
.RS 4
specify parametrisation of GFN\-xTB: 0 (GFN0\-xTB), 1 (GFN1\-xTB),
2 (GFN2\-xTB, the default) or ff (GFN\-FF, the same as \f(CR\-\-gfnff\fP); any
other value is refused with an error. The shorthands \f(CR\-\-gfn0\fP, \f(CR\-\-gfn1\fP
and \f(CR\-\-gfn2\fP select 0, 1 and 2 without an argument.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-gfnff, \-\-gff\fP
.RS 4
specify parametrisation of GFN\-FF
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-iff\fP
.RS 4
use the IFF (intermolecular force field) calculator; the fragment sizes are
taken from \f(CR\-\-nat\fP
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-mcgfnff\fP
.RS 4
use the MC\-GFN\-FF force field
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-nat\fP \fILIST\fP
.RS 4
number of atoms per fragment of the IFF calculation, comma separated
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-tblite\fP
.RS 4
use tblite library as implementation for xTB
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-help\-values\fP
.RS 4
print the legal value set of every documented option as a stable,
line\-oriented table, one record per option in the order of \f(CR\-\-help\fP: a
category key, the spelling line of \f(CR\-\-help\fP (a token that starts with a
dash is an option spelling, any other token is its argument placeholder)
and the space separated legal value set, empty when the option has none,
separated by a TAB.  Lines starting with \f(CR#\fP are comments and fix the
format (format 1).  This is the table a shell completion generator codes
against; \f(CR\-\-help <OPTION>\fP prints the same set as a \f(CRValues:\fP line.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-color\fP \fISCHEME\fP
.RS 4
colour scheme of the tblite output, one of auto (follow the build feature,
default), always or never (tblite builds only)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-ceh <GRAD> [REAL]\fP
.RS 4
calculate CEH (Charge\-Extended H\[u00FC]ckel model) charges and write them to the
ceh.charges file, optionally, calculate numerical gradients and write them
to the ceh.charges.numgrad file with an adjustable step size for the
numerical gradients.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-ptb\fP
.RS 4
performs single\-point calculation with the density tight\-binding method PTB.
Provides electronic structure and properties, such as, e.g., atomic charges,
bond orders, and dipole moments, but does not provide any energy\-related
properties, such as, e.g., total energy, nuclear gradients, or vibrational
frequencies.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-spinpol\fP
.RS 4
enables spin\-polarization for xTB methods (tblite required)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-oniom\fP \fIMETHOD\fP \fILIST\fP
.RS 4
use subtractive embedding via ONIOM method. \fIMETHOD\fP is given as \f(CRhigh:low\fP
where \f(CRhigh\fP can be \fIorca\fP, \fIturbomole\fP, \fIgfn2\fP, \fIgfn1\fP, or \fIgfnff\fP and
\f(CRlow\fP can be \fIgfn2\fP, \fIgfn1\fP, or \fIgfnff\fP. The inner region is given as
comma\-separated indices directly in the commandline or in a file with each
index on a separate line.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-driver\fP [\fIEXECUTABLE\fP]
.RS 4
use an external driver program for the energy and the gradient; the optional
argument names the executable to run (there is no built\-in default)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-orca\fP
.RS 4
use the external ORCA program for the energy and the gradient
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-mopac\fP
.RS 4
use the external MOPAC program for the energy and the gradient
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-tm\fP
.RS 4
use the external Turbomole program for the energy and the gradient
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-efield\fP \fIREAL,REAL,REAL\fP
.RS 4
static electric field in Cartesian coordinates, overrides \f(CR.EFIELD\fP file,
works only via tblite for the xTB Hamiltonians, or with GFN\-FF and PTB.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-etemp\fP \fIREAL\fP
.RS 4
electronic temperature for SCC (default = 300K)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-esp\fP
.RS 4
calculate electrostatic potential on VdW\-grid
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-stm\fP
.RS 4
calculate STM image
.RE
.sp
\fB\-a, \-\-acc\fP \fIREAL\fP
.RS 4
accuracy for SCC calculation, lower is better (default = 1.0)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-iterations, \-\-maxiterations\fP \fIINT\fP
.RS 4
maximum number of SCC iterations per single point calculation (default =
250)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-vparam\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
Parameter file for xTB calculation
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-alpb\fP \fISOLVENT\fP [\fISTATE\fP]
.RS 4
analytical linearized Poisson\-Boltzmann (ALPB) model,
available solvents are \fIacetone\fP, \fIacetonitrile\fP, \fIaniline\fP, \fIbenzaldehyde\fP,
\fIbenzene\fP, \fIch2cl2\fP, \fIchcl3\fP, \fIcs2\fP, \fIdioxane\fP, \fIdmf\fP, \fIdmso\fP, \fIethanol\fP,
\fIether\fP, \fIethylacetate\fP, \fIfurane\fP, \fIhexadecane\fP, \fIhexane\fP, \fImethanol\fP,
\fInitromethane\fP, \fIoctanol\fP, \fIwoctanol\fP, \fIphenol\fP, \fItoluene\fP, \fIthf\fP, \fIwater\fP.
The solvent input is not case\-sensitive.
The Gsolv reference state can be chosen as \fIreference\fP, \fIbar1M\fP, or \fIgsolv\fP
(default).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-g, \-\-gbsa\fP \fISOLVENT\fP [\fISTATE\fP]
.RS 4
generalized born (GB) model with solvent accessable surface (SASA) model,
available solvents are \fIacetone\fP, \fIacetonitrile\fP, \fIbenzene\fP, \fICH2Cl2\fP,
\fICHCl3\fP, \fICS2\fP, \fIDMF\fP (only GFN2\-xTB), \fIDMSO\fP, \fIether\fP, \fIH2O\fP, \fImethanol\fP,
\fIn\-hexane\fP (only GFN2\-xTB), \fITHF\fP and \fItoluene\fP.
The solvent input is not case\-sensitive.
The Gsolv reference state can be chosen as \fIreference\fP, \fIbar1M\fP, or \fIgsolv\fP
(default).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-gbe\fP \fISOLVENT/EPSILON\fP
.RS 4
generalized Born for finite epsilon (GBe) solvation model (tblite required),
includes only the electrostatic solvation contribution (no SASA model),
available solvents are all solvents that are available for alpb.
Additionally, the dielectric constant can be set manually.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-gb\fP \fISOLVENT/EPSILON\fP
.RS 4
generalized Born (GB) solvation model (tblite required),
includes only the electrostatic solvation contribution (no SASA model),
available solvents are all solvents that are available for alpb.
Additionally, the dielectric constant can be set manually.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cosmo\fP \fISOLVENT/EPSILON\fP
.RS 4
domain decomposition conductor\-like screening model (ddCOSMO)
available solvents are all solvents that are available for alpb.
Additionally, the dielectric constant can be set manually or an ideal
conductor can be chosen by setting epsilon to infinity.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-tmcosmo\fP \fISOLVENT/EPSILON\fP
.RS 4
same as \-\-cosmo, but uses TM convention for writing the .cosmo files.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cpcmx\fP \fISOLVENT\fP
.RS 4
extended conduction\-like polarizable continuum solvation model (CPCM\-X),
available solvents are all solvents included in the Minnesota Solvation
Database.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cma\fP
.RS 4
shifts molecule to center of mass and transforms cartesian coordinates
into the coordinate system of the principle axis (not affected by
\f(CRisotopes\fP file).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-pop\fP
.RS 4
requests printout of Mulliken population analysis
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-molden\fP
.RS 4
requests printout of molden file
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-alpha\fP
.RS 4
requests the extension of electrical properties to static molecular dipole
polarizabilities
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-raman\fP [\fITEMP\fP] [\fIWAVELENGTH\fP]
.RS 4
requests Raman spectrum calculation via combination of GFN2\-xTB and PTB,
using the temperature \fITEMP\fP (default 298.15 K) and the wavelength of the
incident laser in nm \fIWAVELENGTH\fP (default 514 nm); PTB needs a tblite
build, otherwise the option is refused
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-dipole\fP
.RS 4
requests dipole printout
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-wbo\fP
.RS 4
requests Wiberg bond order printout
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-wrtopo\fP \fILIST\fP
.RS 4
comma separated topology printouts, from etot, gnorm, nb, bpair, alist,
blist, tlist, vtors, vbond, vangl, hbbond, eeq and json (json selects all
of them); a misspelled keyword aborts the run
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lmo\fP
.RS 4
requests localization of orbitals
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-fod\fP
.RS 4
requests FOD calculation
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-enso\fP
.RS 4
write the machine\-readable thermochemistry xtb_enso.json in a frequency run
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cycles\fP \fIINT\fP
.RS 4
maximum number of optimization cycles
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-method\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
select the method of the current landscape run type; the value domain is
filtered by the run type and an unknown or mismatched name aborts the run.
\f(CR\-\-lopt\fP and \f(CR\-\-lscan\fP accept the minimisers sd, cg, lbfgs, bfgs, diis, rfo;
\f(CR\-\-lts\fP accepts the saddle searches prfo, bprfo, dimer, neb, cineb, autoneb,
string, gsm; \f(CR\-\-lirc\fP accepts the integrators gs, hpc, lqa, eulerpc; \f(CRauto\fP
keeps the default of the task.  With \f(CR\-o, \-\-opt\fP and \f(CR\-\-ohess\fP the native
engines ancopt (alias rf), lancopt (alias l\-ancopt), fire (alias inertial)
and pbc_lbfgs are accepted, and a landscape minimiser selects the suite
that the redeployed \f(CR\-\-opt\fP runs.  \f(CR\-\-method\fP is the canonical name of
\f(CR\-\-lopt\-engine\fP, \f(CR\-\-lscan\-method\fP, \f(CR\-\-lts\-method\fP and \f(CR\-\-lirc\-method\fP.
The name is not case\-sensitive.
.RE
.SS "RUNTYPS"
.RS 4
\fBNOTE:\fP You can only select \fBone\fP runtyp, only the first runtyp will be used
from the program, use implemented composite runtyps to perform several
operations at once.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-scc, \-\-sp\fP
.RS 4
performs a single point calculation
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-vip\fP
.RS 4
performs calculation of ionisation potential.
This needs the param_ipea\-xtb.txt parameters
and a GFN1 Hamiltonian.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-vea\fP
.RS 4
performs calculation of electron affinity.
This needs the param_ipea\-xtb.txt parameters
and a GFN1 Hamiltonian.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-vipea\fP
.RS 4
performs calculation of electron affinity and ionisation potential.
This needs the param_ipea\-xtb.txt parameters
and a GFN1 Hamiltonian.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-vfukui\fP
.RS 4
performs calculation of Fukui indices.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-vomega\fP
.RS 4
performs calculation of electrophilicity index.
This needs the param_ipea\-xtb.txt parameters
and a GFN1 Hamiltonian.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-grad\fP
.RS 4
performs a gradient calculation
.RE
.sp
\fB\-o, \-\-opt\fP [\fILEVEL\fP]
.RS 4
run the landscape suite geometry optimization (pass \f(CR\-\-method ancopt\fP
for the native \f(CRancopt(3)\fP),
levels from crude, sloppy, loose, lax, normal (default), tight, verytight,
vtight, extreme and the suite\-only superloose can be chosen
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lopt\fP
.RS 4
minimise the geometry with the landscape task suite (\f(CRsrc/landscape\fP); the
algorithm is selected with \f(CR\-\-method\fP (alias \f(CR\-\-lopt\-engine\fP) and the
thresholds with \f(CR\-\-lopt\-level\fP.  The sub\-options are in SUITE OPTIONS
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\fP
.RS 4
scan an internal coordinate (distance, angle or dihedral) with the
landscape task suite; the result is written to \f(CRxtbscan.log\fP with the
de\-biased energy of every frame.  The sub\-options are in SUITE OPTIONS
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\fP
.RS 4
transition state search with the landscape task suite; the method is
selected with \f(CR\-\-method\fP (alias \f(CR\-\-lts\-method\fP), one of prfo (default),
bprfo, dimer, neb, cineb, autoneb or string (alias gsm).  Writes \f(CRxtbts.xyz\fP
and \f(CRxtbts.log\fP and confirms the imaginary mode count.  The sub\-options are
in SUITE OPTIONS
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\fP
.RS 4
intrinsic reaction coordinate with the landscape task suite; the input
geometry is the transition state and must have exactly one imaginary mode.
The integrator is selected with \f(CR\-\-method\fP (alias \f(CR\-\-lirc\-method\fP), one of
hpc (default), lqa, eulerpc or gs.  Writes \f(CRirc_forward.xyz\fP,
\f(CRirc_backward.xyz\fP, \f(CRirc_path.xyz\fP and \f(CRirc.log\fP.  The sub\-options are in
SUITE OPTIONS
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lvib\fP
.RS 4
harmonic frequencies, normal modes and thermochemistry with the landscape
task suite; writes \f(CRvibspectrum\fP, \f(CRvib_normal_modes\fP, \f(CRg98.out\fP and
\f(CRhessian\fP with the names and formats of \f(CR\-\-hess\fP.  The sub\-options are in
SUITE OPTIONS
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-ljson\fP
.RS 4
write the structured result \f(CRlandscape_<TASK>.json\fP for every task \- native
(\f(CR\-\-sp\fP, \f(CR\-\-grad\fP, \f(CR\-\-opt\fP, \f(CR\-\-hess\fP, ...) and landscape (\f(CR\-\-lopt\fP,
\f(CR\-\-lscan\fP, \f(CR\-\-lts\fP, \f(CR\-\-lirc\fP, \f(CR\-\-lvib\fP).  The document has a uniform core
block (task, status, energy, geometry, provenance) plus the task specific
counters and convergence history
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-hess\fP
.RS 4
perform a numerical hessian calculation on input geometry
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-ohess\fP [\fILEVEL\fP]
.RS 4
perform a numerical hessian calculation on a geometry optimized by the
landscape suite (pass \f(CR\-\-method ancopt\fP for the native \f(CRancopt(3)\fP
optimizer)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-bhess\fP [\fILEVEL\fP]
.RS 4
perform a biased numerical hessian calculation on an \f(CRancopt(3)\fP optimized
geometry
.RE
.sp
Hessian runs automatically perform a compliance analysis for nonperiodic
systems with more than one atom and no frozen atoms.
.sp
\fB\-\-o1nh\fP
.RS 4
perform the numerical hessian calculation using the ODLR approximation
(O1NumHess)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-imagmin\fP \fIREAL\fP
.RS 4
magnitudes below this value (cm\[u207B]\[u00B9]) are ignored in the O1NumHess
imaginary frequency repair (default 5).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-imagmax\fP \fIREAL\fP
.RS 4
deepest imaginary frequency (cm\[u207B]\[u00B9]) considered in the O1NumHess
imaginary frequency repair (default 200).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-md\fP
.RS 4
molecular dynamics simulation on start geometry
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-metadyn\fP [\fIINT\fP]
.RS 4
meta dynamics simulation on start geometry, saving \fIint\fP snapshots
of the trajectory to bias the simulation
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-bias\-input, \-\-gesc\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
file with the RMSD bias of the metadynamics run (\f(CR\-\-metadyn\fP)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-omd\fP
.RS 4
molecular dynamics simulation on \f(CRancopt(3)\fP optimized geometry,
a loose optimization level will be chosen
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-mdopt\fP
.RS 4
re\-optimise the structures of the molecular dynamics trajectory xtb.trj at
the current optimisation level; the energies are written to xtb_ensemble.xyz
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-screen\fP
.RS 4
optimisation loop over an ensemble: QMDFF generates it when a file
\fIsolvent\fP exists, otherwise it is read from the file xtbscreen.xyz
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-ewin\fP \fIREAL\fP
.RS 4
energy window of the conformational search (default 20.0 Eh)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-metaopt\fP [\fILEVEL\fP]
.RS 4
call \f(CRancopt(3)\fP to perform a geometry optimization,
then try to find other minimas by meta dynamics
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-path\fP [\fIFILE\fP]
.RS 4
use meta dynamics to calculate a path from the input geometry
to the given product structure
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-modef\fP \fIINT\fP
.RS 4
modefollowing algorithm. \fIINT\fP specifies the mode that should be
used for the modefollowing.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-dipro\fP [\fIREAL\fP]
.RS 4
the dimer projection method for the calculation of electronic coupling
integrals between two fragments. \fIREAL\fP sets the threshold for nearly
degenerate orbitals to still be considered (default = 0.1 eV).
.RE
.SH "SUITE OPTIONS"
.sp
The landscape task suite (\f(CRsrc/landscape\fP) is selected by one of the run types
\f(CR\-\-lopt\fP, \f(CR\-\-lscan\fP, \f(CR\-\-lts\fP, \f(CR\-\-lirc\fP or \f(CR\-\-lvib\fP (see RUNTYPS above), and by
the redeployed \f(CR\-\-opt\fP and \f(CR\-\-ohess\fP; the sub\-options below configure the
selected task and the common \f(CR\-\-ljson\fP switch is listed with the run types.
Every sub\-option selects a landscape run type itself, so combining one with a
native run type is a \fBrun type conflict\fP and aborts during argument parsing
(\f(CRoxtb mol.xyz \-\-sp \-\-lscan\-fc 0.05\fP fails with a run type conflict naming
\f(CR\-\-scc\fP and \f(CR\-\-lscan\-fc\fP); the native run types do not ignore these options,
they never get that far. \f(CR\-\-opt\fP and \f(CR\-\-ohess\fP take the suite's \f(CR\-\-method\fP,
\f(CR\-\-cycles\fP, \f(CR\-\-acc\fP and \f(CR\-\-ljson\fP but not the per\-task sub\-options.
.sp
The suite keeps every quantity in atomic units internally and converts at
the input boundary. Unless an option says otherwise a length is in bohr, an
energy in hartree, a gradient or force in hartree/bohr, a Hessian element in
hartree/bohr\u2 and a stiffness in hartree/bohr\d2. The exceptions are
spelled out at their options: a scanned coordinate uses angstrom (distance)
or degree (angle and dihedral), the two dimer lengths and every trust radius
are Cartesian bohr, the IRC arc lengths are in \f(CRbohr*amu^1/2\fP (mass weighted
arc length, one atomic unit) and the frequency cutoffs are in \f(CRcm^\-1\fP.
.sp
\f(CR\-\-method\fP (see OPTIONS) is the canonical spelling of \f(CR\-\-lopt\-engine\fP,
\f(CR\-\-lscan\-method\fP, \f(CR\-\-lts\-method\fP and \f(CR\-\-lirc\-method\fP: the same value through
two of the flags is idempotent, a different value aborts the run, and an
unknown name or a name of another task is an error. Every name is matched
case insensitively.
.SS "MINIMISATION OPTIONS (\-\-lopt)"
.sp
\fB\-\-lopt\-engine\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
minimisation algorithm of the suite, one of \f(CRsd\fP, \f(CRcg\fP, \f(CRlbfgs\fP (default),
\f(CRbfgs\fP, \f(CRdiis\fP or \f(CRrfo\fP. Alias of \f(CR\-\-method\fP; the name is not
case\-sensitive. Because the native \f(CR\-\-opt\fP runs \f(CRancopt(3)\fP, the engine of
the run is announced in one boxed line before the first geometry is
evaluated, marked \f(CR(default)\fP for the builtin default and \f(CR(explicit)\fP for
a name given on the command line.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lopt\-level\fP \fILEVEL\fP
.RS 4
convergence level, one of \f(CRsuperloose\fP, \f(CRloose\fP, \f(CRnormal\fP (default), \f(CRtight\fP
or \f(CRvtight\fP; not case\-sensitive. One level drives four axes at once: the
four thresholds (maximum and RMS gradient in hartree/bohr, maximum and RMS
displacement in bohr), the energy tolerance \f(CRe_tol\fP (hartree), the SCC
accuracy of the engine and the multiplier of the step budget (3 steps per
atom at \f(CRnormal\fP, clamped to 200..10000).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lopt\-hessian\fP \fISOURCE\fP
.RS 4
Hessian backend, one of \f(CRnumerical\fP (default; the suite's own central
differences of the electronic gradient), \f(CRmodel\fP (Lindh\-D2 model Hessian
from \f(CRsrc/model_hessian\fP) or \f(CRfile\fP (read the TURBOMOLE layout written by an
earlier run, see \f(CR\-\-lopt\-hessian\-file\fP). The numerical source costs 6N
displaced single points; one line names that number before the first
displacement starts, and every periodic rebuild announces itself the same
way (see \f(CR\-\-lopt\-recalc\fP). The \f(CRmodel\fP and \f(CRfile\fP sources evaluate nothing
and print no such line.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lopt\-max\-steps\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
step budget of the minimisation. The default is derived from the convergence
level (3 steps per atom, clamped to 200..10000); \f(CR\-\-cycles\fP and the level
change it. A budget given here wins over the level\-derived value.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lopt\-recalc\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
rebuild the exact Hessian through the provider every N RFO steps (default
5); 0 disables the rebuild, so the run keeps the Hessian update alone, and
an interval above the step budget never fires. A negative value is reported
(and fatal with \f(CR\-\-strict\fP) and behaves like 0. Only \f(CR\-\-lopt\-engine rfo\fP
rebuilds at all; the saddle search and the reaction path have their own
\f(CR\-\-lts\-recalc\fP and \f(CR\-\-lirc\-recalc\fP. Each rebuild announces itself with one
line before its displaced single points start.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lopt\-hess\-step\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
finite difference step of the numerical Hessian in bohr (default 0.005).
Only \f(CR\-\-lopt\-engine rfo\fP builds a numerical Hessian; the other five engines
(including the default \f(CRlbfgs\fP) never do, and the value is ignored for
\f(CR\-\-lopt\-hessian model\fP or \f(CRfile\fP. The run then echoes the effective value
and marks it as not used.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lopt\-sccacc\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
SCC accuracy of the displaced single points of the numerical Hessian
(default 0.3). Consumed by the same engine and Hessian source as
\f(CR\-\-lopt\-hess\-step\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lopt\-hess\-jobs\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
number of processes that evaluate the displaced single points of this
task's numerical Hessian at the same time (default 1, the serial loop).
Each worker is a fork of the running program, so it inherits the calculator,
the geometry and the reference wavefunction; the parent combines the
gradient columns, so the count changes the wall time only and the \f(CRhessian\fP
file is byte for byte the same at every count. A single\-threaded run is
required, because a worker inherits the team of the parent:
\f(CROMP_NUM_THREADS=1\fP is the way to say that (and \f(CR\-P 1\fP if
\f(CR\-P\fP/\f(CR\-\-parallel\fP is given). A threaded run is refused instead of nested, a
value above the processor count is reported, and a value below 1 runs
serially with a warning. Ignored by the \f(CRmodel\fP and
\f(CRfile\fP Hessian sources.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lopt\-restart\fP \fIMODE\fP
.RS 4
wavefunction restart policy of the engine, \f(CRoff\fP (default), \f(CRpin\fP or \f(CRwarm\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lopt\-hessian\-file\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
file read by \f(CR\-\-lopt\-hessian file\fP (TURBOMOLE \f(CR$hessian\fP layout, the
\f(CRhessian\fP file written by \f(CR\-\-hess\fP and by \f(CR\-\-lvib\fP). The name is used only
by the \f(CRfile\fP source: with \f(CRnumerical\fP or \f(CRmodel\fP it is silently discarded
and never echoed (a registered, deliberately unfixed honesty gap).
.RE
.SS "SCAN OPTIONS (\-\-lscan)"
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-coord\fP \fITYPE\fP
.RS 4
type of the first scanned coordinate, one of \f(CRdistance\fP (default), \f(CRangle\fP
or \f(CRdihedral\fP; not case\-sensitive.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-atoms\fP \fILIST\fP
.RS 4
1\-based atom indices of the first coordinate, comma or blank separated (two
for a distance, three for an angle, four for a dihedral).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-step\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
step size of the first coordinate in angstrom (distance) or degree (angle
and dihedral).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-nstep\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
number of steps of the first coordinate; a sweep visits the coordinate
values \f(CRq0 + k step\fP for \f(CRk = 0..nstep\fP, in both directions unless
\f(CR\-\-lscan\-oneway\fP is given.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-coord2, \-\-lscan\-atoms2, \-\-lscan\-step2, \-\-lscan\-nstep2\fP
.RS 4
the same four options for a second scanned coordinate; any of them switches
the scan to a two\-dimensional Cartesian product grid (as \f(CR\-\-lscan\-ndim 2\fP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP
.RS 4
scan mode, \f(CRrelaxed\fP (default; every point is minimised under a constraint
potential) or \f(CRrigid\fP (the geometry is constructed and a single point is
evaluated); not case\-sensitive.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-method\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
inner minimiser of a relaxed scan, one of \f(CRsd\fP, \f(CRcg\fP, \f(CRlbfgs\fP (default),
\f(CRbfgs\fP, \f(CRdiis\fP or \f(CRrfo\fP. Alias of \f(CR\-\-method\fP; not case\-sensitive.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-level\fP \fILEVEL\fP
.RS 4
convergence level of the inner minimisation, one of \f(CRsuperloose\fP, \f(CRloose\fP,
\f(CRnormal\fP (default), \f(CRtight\fP or \f(CRvtight\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-ndim\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
number of scanned coordinates, 1 (default) or 2.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-restart\fP \fIMODE\fP
.RS 4
wavefunction policy of the engine at every scan point, \f(CRoff\fP (default),
\f(CRpin\fP or \f(CRwarm\fP. \f(CRoff\fP and \f(CRpin\fP reset the incoming wavefunction from the
stored reference, \f(CRwarm\fP \fBkeeps\fP it, so \f(CRwarm\fP carries the SCF history of
the previous point into the next one (measured: reusing the wavefunction
moves the gradient by about 4e\-7 hartree/bohr, which is why the suite keeps
the policy uniform inside one evaluation sequence). The geometry warm start
is a separate mechanism, \f(CRwarm_start\fP, which is on by default and reuses the
previous point's geometry.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-oneway\fP
.RS 4
scan the forward branch only; no reverse branch and no hysteresis report.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-cold\-start\fP
.RS 4
start every scan point from the input geometry instead of the geometry of
the previous point.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-fc\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
force constant of the constraint bias potential (the coordinate deviation
raised to the power \f(CR\-\-lscan\-expo\fP and scaled by this value) in
hartree/bohr^2 (default 0.5). This is ten times the xtb scan force constant
\f(CRfcconstr = 0.05\fP of the \f(CR$scan\fP block, so the active default is echoed as a
notice unless the flag is given; use \f(CR\-\-lscan\-fc 0.05\fP when compatibility
with existing \f(CRxtbscan.log\fP tool chains matters.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-expo\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
exponent of the constraint potential (default 2.0). Only distance
constraints use it: an angle is biased by the squared angular deviation and
a dihedral by \f(CR(1 \- cos dphi)\fP, both scaled by the same force constant and
bounded by twice it, with a zero torque at 180 degree.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-max\-steps\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
step budget of the inner relaxation of every scan point; derived from the
convergence level like \f(CR\-\-lopt\-max\-steps\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-hess\-step\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
finite difference step of the numerical Hessian of the scan points in bohr
(default 0.005). Only \f(CR\-\-lscan\-method rfo\fP builds one (the default \f(CRlbfgs\fP
never does), a rigid scan has no inner minimisation at all, and the value is
ignored for \f(CR\-\-lscan\-hessian model\fP or \f(CRfile\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-sccacc\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
SCC accuracy of the displaced single points of the numerical Hessian
(default 0.3); consumed by the same inner minimiser and Hessian source as
\f(CR\-\-lscan\-hess\-step\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-hess\-jobs\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
number of processes that evaluate the displaced single points of this
task's numerical Hessian at the same time (default 1, the serial loop).
Each worker is a fork of the running program, so it inherits the calculator,
the geometry and the reference wavefunction; the parent combines the
gradient columns, so the count changes the wall time only and the \f(CRhessian\fP
file is byte for byte the same at every count. A single\-threaded run is
required, because a worker inherits the team of the parent:
\f(CROMP_NUM_THREADS=1\fP is the way to say that (and \f(CR\-P 1\fP if
\f(CR\-P\fP/\f(CR\-\-parallel\fP is given). A threaded run is refused instead of nested, a
value above the processor count is reported, and a value below 1 runs
serially with a warning. Ignored by the \f(CRmodel\fP and
\f(CRfile\fP Hessian sources.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-hessian\fP \fISOURCE\fP
.RS 4
Hessian backend of the scan points, one of \f(CRnumerical\fP (default), \f(CRmodel\fP or
\f(CRfile\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lscan\-hessian\-file\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
file read by \f(CR\-\-lscan\-hessian file\fP (TURBOMOLE layout); silently discarded
when the source is not \f(CRfile\fP.
.RE
.SS "TRANSITION STATE OPTIONS (\-\-lts)"
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-method\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
search method, one of \f(CRprfo\fP (default), \f(CRbprfo\fP (restricted step), \f(CRdimer\fP
(first derivatives only, no Hessian), \f(CRneb\fP, \f(CRcineb\fP (climbing image NEB),
\f(CRautoneb\fP (incremental path building) or \f(CRstring\fP/\f(CRgsm\fP (growing string).
Alias of \f(CR\-\-method\fP; not case\-sensitive. \f(CRneb\fP, \f(CRcineb\fP, \f(CRautoneb\fP, \f(CRstring\fP
and \f(CRgsm\fP are chain\-of\-states methods and need \f(CR\-\-lts\-product\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-trust\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
initial trust radius of the step in bohr (default 0.37795).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-trust\-max\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
upper bound of the trust radius in bohr (default 1.8897).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-hlow\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
curvature floor of the downhill modes in hartree/bohr^2 (default 0.01).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-max\-steps\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
maximum number of macro iterations; derived from the convergence level (3
steps per atom, clamped to 200..10000).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-level\fP \fILEVEL\fP
.RS 4
convergence level (thresholds, energy tolerance, SCC accuracy and step
budget), one of \f(CRsuperloose\fP, \f(CRloose\fP, \f(CRnormal\fP (default), \f(CRtight\fP or
\f(CRvtight\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-mode\-overlap\-min\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
mode following guard: reselect the transition vector below this overlap
(default 0.3). The value is the dimensionless \f(CR|V^T v|\fP overlap.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-alpha\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
fraction of the trust radius assigned to the uphill subspace by \f(CRbprfo\fP
(default 0.5).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-micro\-cycles\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
maximum number of restricted step micro iterations (default 50).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-target\-mode\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
which negative mode is followed, 1 = most negative (default 1).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-init\-mode\fP \fIMODE\fP
.RS 4
initial transition vector, \f(CRh0\fP (most negative Hessian mode, default) or
\f(CRforce\fP (largest Cartesian gradient component direction).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-escape\-step\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
displacement along the lowest internal mode in bohr (default 0.2), applied
when the start geometry is a minimum so that P\-RFO has a direction to ascend
along; 0 disables the escape. The active default is echoed as a notice
unless the flag is given. The full walk budget is 3.0 bohr.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-delta\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
dimer separation, the distance of each image from the common midpoint in
bohr (default 0.018897, i.e. 0.01 angstrom; this is the pysisyphus
convention, not the VASP \f(CRDdR\fP that denotes twice the value).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-dr\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
finite difference step of the dimer translation probe in bohr (default
0.0018897).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-rot\-max\-iter\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
maximum number of dimer rotations per macro iteration (default 5).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-rot\-alpha\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
damping of the dimer rotation angle (default 0.5; 1 = undamped).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-rot\-frms\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
RMS torque below which the dimer rotation has converged, in hartree/bohr
(default 2.65e\-4).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-ninit\fP \fIMODE\fP
.RS 4
initial dimer axis, \f(CRrandom\fP (default, seeded by \f(CR\-\-lts\-seed\fP) or \f(CRforce\fP
(largest Cartesian gradient component direction).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-seed\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
seed of the random initial dimer axis (default 42). The generator state is
reset from this seed before every draw, so a run is exactly reproducible.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-step\-max\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
maximum dimer translation step in bohr (default 0.28346).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-step0\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
upper bound of the first dimer translation in bohr (default 0.37795); the
\f(CRdR\fP curvature probe can shorten the actual first displacement.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-translate\-max\-iter\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
maximum number of quick\-min displacement updates per dimer translation
(default 1).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-recalc\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
recompute the exact Hessian every N macro steps, 0 disables (default 1).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-no\-verify\fP
.RS 4
skip the frequency confirmation of the located stationary point.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-no\-rigid\fP
.RS 4
keep the rigid body (translation and rotation) components in the Cartesian
step instead of projecting them out.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-restart\fP \fIMODE\fP
.RS 4
wavefunction restart policy of the engine, \f(CRoff\fP (default), \f(CRpin\fP or \f(CRwarm\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-hessian\fP \fISOURCE\fP
.RS 4
Hessian backend, one of \f(CRnumerical\fP (default), \f(CRmodel\fP (Lindh\-D2) or \f(CRfile\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-hessian\-file\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
file read by \f(CR\-\-lts\-hessian file\fP (TURBOMOLE layout); silently discarded
when the source is not \f(CRfile\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-hess\-step\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
finite difference step of the numerical confirmation Hessian in bohr
(default 0.005); the canonical name of \f(CR\-\-lts\-step\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-step\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
alias of \f(CR\-\-lts\-hess\-step\fP. If the confirmed mode lies within
\f(CR0 +/\- 3 cm^\-1\fP, the curvature sign is re\-decided with 0.002 bohr and SCC
accuracy 0.1.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-sccacc\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
SCC accuracy of the displaced single points of the confirmation Hessian
(default 0.3).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-hess\-jobs\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
number of processes that evaluate the displaced single points of this
task's numerical Hessian at the same time (default 1, the serial loop).
Each worker is a fork of the running program, so it inherits the calculator,
the geometry and the reference wavefunction; the parent combines the
gradient columns, so the count changes the wall time only and the \f(CRhessian\fP
file is byte for byte the same at every count. A single\-threaded run is
required, because a worker inherits the team of the parent:
\f(CROMP_NUM_THREADS=1\fP is the way to say that (and \f(CR\-P 1\fP if
\f(CR\-P\fP/\f(CR\-\-parallel\fP is given). A threaded run is refused instead of nested, a
value above the processor count is reported, and a value below 1 runs
serially with a warning. Ignored by the \f(CRmodel\fP and
\f(CRfile\fP Hessian sources.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-product\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
product endpoint of a chain\-of\-states search (\f(CRneb\fP, \f(CRcineb\fP, \f(CRautoneb\fP,
\f(CRstring\fP, \f(CRgsm\fP): a single frame xyz with the same atoms in the same order;
the positional input file is the reactant.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-images\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
number of images of a band, the two fixed endpoints included (default 9);
for \f(CRautoneb\fP it is the requested final number of images.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-kmin, \-\-lts\-kmax\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
lower and upper spring constant of the dynamic springs in hartree/bohr^2
(defaults 1e\-2 and 1e\-1, calibrated on the analytic LEPS surface).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-kdecay\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
decay factor of the dynamic spring formula (default 0.5).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-no\-dynamic\-k\fP
.RS 4
use the single spring constant \f(CRkmax\fP for every image.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-climb\fP
.RS 4
switch on the climbing image stage (CI\-NEB); implied by
\f(CR\-\-lts\-method cineb\fP, \f(CRautoneb\fP, \f(CRstring\fP and \f(CRgsm\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-climb\-switch\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
band force in hartree/bohr below which the climbing image is ignited
(default 9.7234e\-3, the standard 0.5 eV/angstrom).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-no\-idpp\fP
.RS 4
straight linear interpolation instead of the IDPP initial path.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-no\-align\fP
.RS 4
do not Kabsch align the images before every band evaluation.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-relax\-ends\fP
.RS 4
relax the two endpoints with the minimiser before the search.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-max\-displ\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
upper bound of one displacement component of a band step in bohr
(default 0.37795).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-frms\-avg, \-\-lts\-frms\-max\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
band convergence thresholds of the average and the largest per\-image force
RMS in hartree/bohr (defaults 3e\-4 and 1e\-3).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-autoneb\-nstart\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
number of images of the coarse start path of an AutoNEB run (default 5).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-autoneb\-max\-paths\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
hard upper bound of the number of images of an AutoNEB path (default 10); a
request above it is an error, never a silent truncation.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-autoneb\-max\-depth\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
hard upper bound of the number of image insertion stages (default 5).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-autoneb\-restart\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
multi\-frame xyz path of an earlier AutoNEB run (its \f(CRpath_autoneb.xyz\fP),
adopted as the initial path so the completed stages are not repeated.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-string\-nnodes\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
target number of nodes of the growing string, endpoints included (default
9). The string is configured here, not by \f(CR\-\-lts\-images\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-string\-grow\-tol\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
norm threshold of the growth indicator in hartree/bohr (default 5e\-3).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-string\-ds\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
arc length increment of one growth step of the string in bohr (default 0.5).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-string\-nu2\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
curvature floor for the node spacing condition, in hartree/bohr^2
(default 0.05).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-string\-grow\-steps\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
maximum perpendicular relaxation iterations of one frontier node (default
200).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-no\-string\-spline\fP
.RS 4
central difference tangents of the string instead of the cubic spline
tangent.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lts\-string\-history\fP
.RS 4
print the growth history (position and perpendicular force of every node);
also honoured by the \f(CRgsm\fP task name.
.RE
.SS "INTRINSIC REACTION COORDINATE OPTIONS (\-\-lirc)"
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-method\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
integration method, one of \f(CRhpc\fP (Hessian based predictor\-corrector,
default), \f(CRlqa\fP (Page\-McIver analytic quadratic propagation, the cheapest),
\f(CReulerpc\fP (Euler predictor\-corrector) or \f(CRgs\fP (Gonzalez\-Schlegel sphere
relaxation). Alias of \f(CR\-\-method\fP; not case\-sensitive. The integrator is
announced in the startup boxed line, marked \f(CR(default)\fP or \f(CR(explicit)\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-step\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
step length of one macro step in \f(CRbohr*amu^1/2\fP (mass weighted arc length,
default 0.10; use 0.05 for a tight path). The clean range is 0.05\-0.15 for
\f(CR\-\-lirc\-method gs\fP, whose macro step stalls in a two cycle from 0.20 on;
\f(CRhpc\fP and \f(CReulerpc\fP are correct at 0.20. The limit cycle guard only detects
the cancelling subclass of the stall: a large step can still end far from
the minimum (measured 0.236 bohr at 0.40 forward).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-max\-steps\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
maximum number of macro steps of one direction; derived from the convergence
level (3 steps per atom, clamped to 200..10000).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-level\fP \fILEVEL\fP
.RS 4
convergence level of the endpoint relaxation (thresholds, energy tolerance,
SCC accuracy and step budget), one of \f(CRsuperloose\fP, \f(CRloose\fP, \f(CRnormal\fP
(default), \f(CRtight\fP or \f(CRvtight\fP. The IRC path itself is left by the
termination signals \f(CR\-\-lirc\-grad\-tol\fP and \f(CR\-\-lirc\-e\-rise\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-swap\fP
.RS 4
exchange the forward and the backward direction defined by the phase
convention.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-oneway\fP
.RS 4
integrate the forward direction only.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-target\-mode\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
which negative mode defines the initial direction, 1 = most negative
(default 1).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-micro\-cycles\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
maximum number of Gonzalez\-Schlegel micro cycles per macro step (default
20).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-micro\-thresh\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
convergence threshold of the sphere relaxation in \f(CRbohr*amu^1/2\fP (default
1e\-3).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-euler\-n\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
Euler/Simpson substeps of the analytic LQA propagation (default 5000).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-loose\-cycles\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
EulerPC coarse phase: use the plain Euler prediction for the first N macro
steps, 0 disables the coarse phase (default 3).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-mbs\-tol\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
acceptance threshold of the modified Bulirsch\-Stoer corrector in
\f(CRbohr*amu^1/2\fP (default 1e\-5; the HPC paper uses 1e\-6).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-mbs\-max\-k\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
maximum extrapolation level of the modified Bulirsch\-Stoer corrector
(default 15).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-mbs\-points\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
base number of Euler substeps of the modified Bulirsch\-Stoer corrector
(default 20).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-dwi\-n\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
capacity of the DWI surface fit (default 4). The implemented interpolant is
two point by construction, so every N >= 2 is behaviourally equivalent; N <
2 is rejected.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-e\-rise\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
termination signal (c): stop after N consecutive rising energies (default
3). A large N does not guarantee running to the step budget; the limit cycle
guard \f(CR\-\-lirc\-osc\-window\fP is an independent signal.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-erise\-steps\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
alias of \f(CR\-\-lirc\-e\-rise\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-grad\-tol\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
termination signal (b): stop when the RMS gradient per Cartesian component
falls below this value in hartree/bohr (default 1e\-3).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-osc\-window\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
limit cycle guard of the \f(CRgs\fP macro step: leave the path after N consecutive
macro steps that cancel each other (default 8; 0 disables the guard). The
guard is inert on every validated path and truncates the stalled walk; the
endpoint it returns is one phase of the oscillation, about one step length
away from the minimum, so the run is reported as incomplete. Only
\f(CR\-\-lirc\-method gs\fP is affected.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-adaptive\fP
.RS 4
opt\-in adaptive step length of the \f(CRgs\fP macro step (default off): halve the
effective step length (floor: a sixteenth of \f(CR\-\-lirc\-step\fP) at the first
cancelling macro step pair. It changes the validated small step paths and is
therefore a re\-baseline; every reduction is printed. Only \f(CR\-\-lirc\-method gs\fP
honours it \- every other method silently ignores it.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-recalc\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
rebuild the exact Hessian every N macro steps, \-1 disables (default \-1).
Pure Bofill updates are sufficient for short paths; long or strongly curved
paths drift and need an explicit \f(CR\-\-lirc\-recalc 5 .. 10\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-no\-endpoint\-relax\fP
.RS 4
do not relax the two endpoints to assign them to a minimum; the
\f(CR\-\-lirc\-rmsd\-end\fP criterion is then skipped (and reported as skipped).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-endpoint\-relax\fP
.RS 4
relax the two endpoints to assign them to a minimum (default; the explicit
flag restores it after a \f(CR\-\-lirc\-no\-endpoint\-relax\fP).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-rmsd\-end\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
Kabsch aligned RMSD between an endpoint and its relaxed minimum below which
the endpoint counts as having reached a minimum, in bohr (default 0.18897).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-hessian\fP \fISOURCE\fP
.RS 4
Hessian backend, one of \f(CRnumerical\fP (default), \f(CRmodel\fP (Lindh\-D2) or \f(CRfile\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-hessian\-file\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
file read by \f(CR\-\-lirc\-hessian file\fP (TURBOMOLE layout); silently discarded
when the source is not \f(CRfile\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-hess\-step\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
finite difference step of the numerical Hessian in bohr (default 0.005); the
canonical name of \f(CR\-\-lirc\-step\-hess\fP, not to be confused with \f(CR\-\-lirc\-step\fP
(the arc length of the reaction path).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-step\-hess\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
alias of \f(CR\-\-lirc\-hess\-step\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-sccacc\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
SCC accuracy of the displaced single points of the Hessian (default 0.3).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-hess\-jobs\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
number of processes that evaluate the displaced single points of this
task's numerical Hessian at the same time (default 1, the serial loop).
Each worker is a fork of the running program, so it inherits the calculator,
the geometry and the reference wavefunction; the parent combines the
gradient columns, so the count changes the wall time only and the \f(CRhessian\fP
file is byte for byte the same at every count. A single\-threaded run is
required, because a worker inherits the team of the parent:
\f(CROMP_NUM_THREADS=1\fP is the way to say that (and \f(CR\-P 1\fP if
\f(CR\-P\fP/\f(CR\-\-parallel\fP is given). A threaded run is refused instead of nested, a
value above the processor count is reported, and a value below 1 runs
serially with a warning. Ignored by the \f(CRmodel\fP and
\f(CRfile\fP Hessian sources.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lirc\-restart\fP \fIMODE\fP
.RS 4
wavefunction restart policy of the engine, \f(CRoff\fP (default), \f(CRpin\fP or \f(CRwarm\fP.
.RE
.SS "FREQUENCY AND THERMOCHEMISTRY OPTIONS (\-\-lvib)"
.sp
\fB\-\-lvib\-hessian\fP \fISOURCE\fP
.RS 4
Hessian backend of the frequency task, one of \f(CRnumerical\fP (default), \f(CRmodel\fP
(Lindh\-D2) or \f(CRfile\fP (read the Hessian of an earlier run).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lvib\-hessian\-file\fP \fINAME\fP
.RS 4
file read by \f(CR\-\-lvib\-hessian file\fP (TURBOMOLE layout). Unlike the other
tasks, \f(CR\-\-lvib\fP prints the name even when the source is not \f(CRfile\fP, but it
still only reads it for \f(CRfile\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lvib\-hess\-step\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
finite difference step of the numerical Hessian in bohr (default 0.005); the
canonical name of \f(CR\-\-lvib\-step\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lvib\-step\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
alias of \f(CR\-\-lvib\-hess\-step\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lvib\-sccacc\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
SCC accuracy of the displaced single points of the numerical Hessian
(default 0.3).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lvib\-hess\-jobs\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
number of processes that evaluate the displaced single points of this
task's numerical Hessian at the same time (default 1, the serial loop).
Each worker is a fork of the running program, so it inherits the calculator,
the geometry and the reference wavefunction; the parent combines the
gradient columns, so the count changes the wall time only and the \f(CRhessian\fP
file is byte for byte the same at every count. A single\-threaded run is
required, because a worker inherits the team of the parent:
\f(CROMP_NUM_THREADS=1\fP is the way to say that (and \f(CR\-P 1\fP if
\f(CR\-P\fP/\f(CR\-\-parallel\fP is given). A threaded run is refused instead of nested, a
value above the processor count is reported, and a value below 1 runs
serially with a warning. Ignored by the \f(CRmodel\fP and
\f(CRfile\fP Hessian sources.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lvib\-restart\fP \fIMODE\fP
.RS 4
wavefunction restart policy of the engine, \f(CRoff\fP (default), \f(CRpin\fP or \f(CRwarm\fP.
The finite difference sequence resets the wavefunction to the state it
entered the Hessian with before every displacement, so \f(CRwarm\fP and \f(CRpin\fP hand
the provider the same start and produce a byte identical \f(CRhessian\fP file;
only \f(CRoff\fP is distinguishable.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lvib\-odlr\fP
.RS 4
request the ODLR approximation. Not implemented: the run reports the request
and the dense provider refuses it \- the Hessian is always built in full.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lvib\-no\-project\fP
.RS 4
do not project the translations and rotations out of the Hessian.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lvib\-temp\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
temperature of the thermodynamic table in K (default 298.15). An explicit
value overrides the xcontrol \f(CR$thermo temp\fP key, which overrides the builtin
default.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lvib\-rotor\-cutoff\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
rotor cutoff of the quasi\-RRHO entropy treatment in \f(CRcm^\-1\fP (default 50.0;
the xcontrol \f(CR$thermo sthr\fP key).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lvib\-imag\-cutoff\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
imaginary frequency cutoff in \f(CRcm^\-1\fP (default \-20.0; the minus sign is
required). The value sets both the mode counting and, together with the
xcontrol \f(CR$thermo imagthr\fP key, the partition function of the
thermochemistry.
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-lvib\-freq\-scal\fP \fIVALUE\fP
.RS 4
frequency scaling factor of the thermochemistry (default 1.0; the xcontrol
\f(CR$thermo scale\fP key).
.RE
.SS "GENERAL"
.sp
\fB\-I, \-\-input\fP \fIFILE\fP
.RS 4
use \fIFILE\fP as input source for \f(CRxcontrol(7)\fP instructions
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-namespace\fP \fISTRING\fP
.RS 4
give this \f(CRoxtb(1)\fP run a namespace. All files, even temporary
ones, will be named according to \fISTRING\fP (might not work everywhere).
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-copy, \-\-nocopy\fP
.RS 4
copies the \f(CRxcontrol\fP file at startup (default = true); \f(CR\-\-nocopy\fP
disables it
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-restart, \-\-norestart\fP
.RS 4
restarts calculation from \f(CRxtbrestart\fP (default = true); \f(CR\-\-norestart\fP
disables it
.RE
.sp
\fB\-P, \-\-parallel\fP \fIINT\fP
.RS 4
number of OpenMP threads of the run, applied before the first parallel
region so that it wins over \f(CROMP_NUM_THREADS\fP; \fIINT\fP must be a positive
integer.  Any other value is reported and the run keeps the team it would
have used without the flag (the OpenMP runtime maps a non\-positive value
to a single thread), and it is fatal with \f(CR\-\-strict\fP.  A value above the
processor count of the machine is reported as well, but the value is
applied as given and never clamped
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-blas\-threads\fP \fIN\fP
.RS 4
set the number of threads used by the linear algebra backend (OpenBLAS or
MKL) at run time and print the value read back; without the flag the
backend is left untouched
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-define\fP
.RS 4
performs automatic check of input and terminate
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-json\fP
.RS 4
write xtbout.json file
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-citation\fP
.RS 4
print citation and terminate
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-license\fP
.RS 4
print license and terminate
.RE
.sp
\fB\-v, \-\-verbose\fP
.RS 4
be more verbose (not supported in every unit)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-V, \-\-very\-verbose\fP
.RS 4
be very verbose (this also enables the verbose output)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-version\fP
.RS 4
print the version banner and terminate
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-nocellopt\fP
.RS 4
do not optimise the lattice of a periodic system, the cell is kept fixed
(default: the cell is optimised)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-ceasefiles\fP
.RS 4
reduce the amount of output and files written (e.g. xtbtopo.mol)
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-strict\fP
.RS 4
turns all warnings into hard errors
.RE
.sp
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fP
.RS 4
show help page
.RE
.sp
\fB\-\-cut\fP
.RS 4
create inner region for \f(CRoniom\fP calculation without performing any
calcultion
.RE
.SH "ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES"
.sp
\f(CRoxtb(1)\fP accesses a path\-like variable to determine the location of its
parameter files, you have to provide the \f(CRXTBPATH\fP variable in the same
syntax as the system \f(CRPATH\fP variable. When \f(CRXTBPATH\fP is unset or empty the
program derives its own parameter directory from the running executable,
testing the install layout \f(CRbindir/../share/o\-xtb\fP and then the build layout
\f(CRbindir/share/o\-xtb\fP, so a
relocated install keeps working without any environment variable. That
directory is prepended to the deprecated \f(CRXTBHOME\fP fallback, and \f(CRXTBHOME\fP
itself falls back to \f(CRHOME\fP, so the full precedence is explicit \f(CRXTBPATH\fP,
\f(CRXTBHOME\fP with the executable\-relative directory in front, then \f(CRHOME\fP.
Every method except the first still needs on\-disk parameter files, which
only \f(CR\-\-gfn 0\fP (GFN0\-xTB) requires at run time \- every other Hamiltonian
carries its parameters in the binary \- so \f(CR\-\-gfn 0\fP works from any directory
of an installed tree with \f(CRXTBPATH\fP unset.
.sp
Since the \f(CRXTBHOME\fP variable is deprecated with version 6.0 and newer
\f(CRoxtb(1)\fP will issue a warning if \f(CRXTBHOME\fP is not part of the \f(CRXTBPATH\fP
since the \f(CRXTBHOME\fP variable is not used in production runs.
.sp
\f(CROMP_NUM_THREADS\fP sets the number of OpenMP threads. A threaded BLAS is the
largest performance trap on multi core machines: the OpenMP threads and the BLAS
thread pool multiply, and an idle pool spins between calls. Measured on GFN2
single points (24 and 501 atoms) at \f(CROMP_NUM_THREADS=8\fP, five runs with the
minimum taken on an idle machine, the settings that recover the loss differ in
strength, and the strongest one comes first:
.IP "1." 4
\fBbefore the process starts\fP, \f(CRexport OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1\fP (12 ms). The
environment variable is consumed while the OpenBLAS pool is created, in an ELF
constructor that runs before \f(CRmain()\fP, so the shell has to export it \- the
program cannot set it in time.
.IP "2." 4
Next, \fB\-\-blas\-threads\fP \fIN\fP together with \f(CROMP_WAIT_POLICY=PASSIVE\fP (20 ms;
both are read at start\-up as well). The flag alone measures 99 ms, 8.3x the
environment variable: it resizes the work partition of the pool through the
runtime API, but the pool itself was already built at eight threads before
\f(CRmain()\fP, and what remains is the OpenMP teams spinning between the calls,
which is exactly what the wait policy and the spin count control. Leaving
everything at its default measures 583 ms.
.IP "3." 4
\f(CROPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1\fP also wins on a larger system. The gain falls with
size \- about 49x at 24 atoms against about 2.8x at 501 atoms \- but at 501
atoms the environment variable still measures 656 ms against 1827 ms with the
pool left at the thread count, while \fB\-\-blas\-threads 1\fP with
\f(CROMP_WAIT_POLICY=PASSIVE\fP measures 699 ms and \fB\-\-blas\-threads 1\fP alone
946 ms.
.sp
\f(CRMKL_NUM_THREADS\fP is ignored by a build linked against OpenBLAS and
\f(CROPENBLAS_DEFAULT_NUM_THREADS\fP measured a no\-op.
.sp
The \f(CR\-\-help\fP page is coloured on a terminal. \f(CRCLICOLOR_FORCE\fP set to a value
other than \f(CR0\fP forces colour even into a pipe and wins over everything else;
otherwise \f(CRNO_COLOR\fP set to a non\-empty value disables it; otherwise colour
follows the output terminal, so a piped or redirected page carries no escape
sequence and is byte\-for\-byte what it is without colour. The colour is
decoration only: stripping the escape sequences reproduces the plain page
exactly.
.sp
The same limit can be requested from inside the program, without any
environment variable, with \fB\-\-blas\-threads\fP \fIN\fP: it calls the OpenBLAS (or
MKL) runtime API through \f(CRdlopen\fP/\f(CRdlsym\fP, prints the thread count it read back
from the backend and warns with \f(CRno OpenBLAS or MKL symbol found\fP when the
linked backend exports no such symbol. That warning also covers a
\fBstatically\fP linked BLAS: its symbols exist only in \f(CR.symtab\fP, which is never
part of the dynamic lookup scope, so the flag has no effect there although the
symbol is present in the binary. Without the flag \f(CRoxtb(1)\fP changes nothing.
.sp
Both API pairs are resolved by symbol name: \f(CRopenblas_set_num_threads\fP with its
getter \f(CRopenblas_get_num_threads\fP, and the oneMKL pair \f(CRmkl_set_num_threads\fP /
\f(CRmkl_get_max_threads\fP (the function \f(CRthreadpoolctl\fP reads MKL's thread count
with). MKL support is therefore written against the documented oneMKL names; no
MKL is installed on the machine this option was developed on, so the MKL branch
has never been executed against a real oneMKL; it was exercised with stub
symbols only, and the OpenBLAS branch is the one verified by running it.
\fB\-\-blas\-threads\fP and \fB\-P, \-\-parallel\fP are different knobs (\fB\-\-parallel\fP sets the
OpenMP thread count and, under an MKL build, the MKL pool as well); where both
reach the same pool the flag that appears last on the command line wins.
.sp
\f(CROMP_STACKSIZE\fP sets the stack size of the OpenMP worker threads \- not the stack
of the main thread, and not the same setting as \f(CRulimit \-s\fP, although the
process stack limit does size the worker stacks too: measured, the worker
threads get 2 MiB while \f(CRulimit \-s\fP is \f(CRunlimited\fP (glibc falls back to the
pthread default) and the stock \f(CRulimit \-s 8192\fP gives them 8 MiB, so the
\f(CRunlimited\fP setting is the smaller of the two. This matters because the GFN0
charge model can need a worker stack that grows with the system size: in the
OpenMP loop over the Coulomb derivative \f(CRgetCoulombDerivsCluster\fP, the array
reduction \f(CRreduction(+:djdr, djdtr, djdL)\fP makes the compiler place each
thread's private copy of the reduction arrays on the thread stack,
\f(CR24*nat*(nat+1) + 96*(nat+1)\fP bytes for \f(CRnat\fP atoms, about \f(CR24*nat^2\fP bytes \- 6
060 096 bytes, about 6 MB, at 500 atoms. When that does not fit, the thread
writes into its guard page and the process dies with \f(CRSIGSEGV\fP. There is no
warning and no usable backtrace: measured, the run is completely silent at the
smallest sizes that crash (0 bytes on stderr), and the single frame the runtime
resolves is \f(CRgomp_thread_start\fP inside libgomp. The size at which this happens
is a property of the environment and not of the code: measured with two threads,
the first size that crashes is about 290 atoms with a 2 MiB worker stack and
about 588 atoms with the stock 8 MiB one, while the formula above is the
portable statement. \f(CR\-\-gfn 0\fP reaches the limit first (GFN1, GFN2 and GFN\-FF ran
clean at 500 and 598 atoms, and GFN2 also at 1001 atoms, with the same thread
counts), but this is a stack size issue and not a method issue: with
\f(CRulimit \-s 4096\fP even a single threaded run of the same system crashes. The
workaround is \f(CRexport OMP_STACKSIZE=512M\fP, or a larger process stack limit such
as \f(CRulimit \-s 65536\fP; it was verified at 290 and 500 atoms and with the official
6.7.1 binary, where the parallel run then reproduces the serial energy. A fixed
generous value is enough, because the requirement grows with the atom count;
\fB\-\-help\fP suggests \f(CRexport OMP_STACKSIZE=4G\fP.
.SH "SHELL COMPLETION"
.sp
The source tree ships three completion scripts in \f(CRcompletions/\fP: \f(CRoxtb.bash\fP
for bash, \f(CR_oxtb\fP for zsh and \f(CRoxtb.fish\fP for fish. They complete the option
spellings, the legal values of every option that has a fixed value set \- for
example \f(CR\-\-lopt\-level\fP completes \f(CRsuperloose\fP, \f(CRloose\fP, \f(CRnormal\fP, \f(CRtight\fP and
\f(CRvtight\fP \- the file names of the geometry argument and of the options that
read a file, and nothing at all for an option whose argument is a number.
.sp
Only the space separated value syntax is completed. The argument parser reads
\f(CR\-\-gfn 1\fP, while a \f(CR\-\-gfn=1\fP is neither a flag nor a file name for it and is
silently ignored, so the completed form is always the separated one.
.sp
The three files are generated from the \f(CR\-\-help\fP option table of \f(CRoxtb(1)\fP by
\f(CRcompletions/tools/gen_completions.py\fP (stdlib only) and committed; the test
\f(CRxtb/landscape\-completions\fP turns red when an option changes without them
being regenerated. \f(CRinstall.sh\fP writes them into the per\-user locations
\f(CR~/.local/share/bash\-completion/completions/oxtb\fP,
\f(CR~/.local/share/zsh/site\-functions/_oxtb\fP and
\f(CR~/.config/fish/completions/oxtb.fish\fP; the package install puts the same
three files under the prefix, in \f(CRshare/bash\-completion/completions\fP,
\f(CRshare/zsh/site\-functions\fP and \f(CRshare/fish/vendor_completions.d\fP.
.sp
Neither the bash\-completion package nor a writable directory that already sits
in zsh's search path is assumed, so \f(CRinstall.sh\fP prints the lines to add after
installing the files:
.IP \(bu 2
\f(CRfpath+=(~/.local/share/zsh/site\-functions)\fP for zsh. Its position matters:
\f(CRcompinit\fP reads \f(CRfpath\fP when it runs, and a shell framework such as
oh\-my\-zsh runs \f(CRcompinit\fP from its own \f(CRsource\fP line, so the line belongs
\fBabove\fP whichever of the two \f(CR~/.zshrc\fP contains \- appending it at the end of
the file registers \f(CR_oxtb\fP for nothing and the tab key falls back to file
names.
.IP \(bu 2
\f(CRsource ~/.local/share/bash\-completion/completions/oxtb\fP for bash, to be put
into \f(CR~/.bashrc\fP; the script is self contained and does not need any helper
of the bash\-completion package. fish reads its own completion directory and
needs no line at all.
.sp
When the completion initialisation cannot be moved below the \f(CRfpath\fP line,
the two commands below it do the same job: \f(CRautoload \-Uz _oxtb\fP makes the
function loadable from the \f(CRfpath\fP directory, and \f(CRcompdef _oxtb oxtb\fP binds
it to the command. The \f(CRautoload\fP is not optional \- without it the shell looks
for an external command \f(CR_oxtb\fP and fails with \f(CRcommand not found\fP.
.sp
\f(CRinstall.sh\fP never edits a shell start\-up file on its own. The explicit opt\-in
is the environment variable \f(CRXTB_PATCH_ZSHRC=ON\fP: it writes a marked,
idempotent block that holds the \f(CRfpath\fP line above the first line of
\f(CR~/.zshrc\fP that initialises completion \- a \f(CRcompinit\fP call, or the \f(CRsource\fP
line of a framework such as oh\-my\-zsh \- after copying that file to
\f(CR~/.zshrc.bak.<timestamp>\fP. The three
completion files are installed unless \f(CRXTB_REGISTER_COMPLETION=OFF\fP is given,
and \f(CRXTB_COMPLETIONS_ONLY=ON\fP refreshes them without running a build.
.SH "LOCAL FILES"
.sp
\f(CRoxtb(1)\fP accesses a number of local files in the current working directory
and also writes some output in specific files. Note that not all input
and output files allow the \fB\-\-namespace\fP option.
.SS "INPUT"
.sp
\fB.CHRG\fP
.RS 4
molecular charge as \fIint\fP
.RE
.sp
\fB.UHF\fP
.RS 4
Number of unpaired electrons as \fIint\fP
.RE
.sp
\fBmdrestart\fP
.RS 4
contains restart information for MD, \fB\-\-namespace\fP compatible.
.RE
.sp
\fBpcharge\fP
.RS 4
point charge input, format is \fIreal\fP \fIreal\fP \fIreal\fP \fIreal\fP [\fIint\fP].
The first real is used as partial charge, the next three entries
are the cartesian coordinates and the last is an optional atom type.
Note that the point charge input is not affected by a CMA transformation.
Also parallel Hessian calculations will fail due to I/O errors when using
point charge embedding.
.RE
.sp
\fBxcontrol\fP
.RS 4
default input file in \fB\-\-copy\fP mode, see \f(CRxcontrol(7)\fP for details,
set by \fB\-\-input\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fBxtbrestart\fP
.RS 4
contains restart information for SCC, \fB\-\-namespace\fP compatible.
.RE
.SS "OUTPUT"
.sp
\fBcharges\fP
.RS 4
contains Mulliken partial charges calculated in SCC
.RE
.sp
\fBceh.charges\fP
.RS 4
contains CEH (Charge\-Extended H\[u00FC]ckel) charges
.RE
.sp
\fBwbo\fP
.RS 4
contains Wiberg bond order calculated in SCC, \fB\-\-namespace\fP compatible.
.RE
.sp
\fBenergy\fP
.RS 4
total energy in Turbomole format
.RE
.sp
\fBgradient\fP
.RS 4
geometry, energy and gradient in Turbomole format
.RE
.sp
\fBhessian\fP
.RS 4
contains the (not mass weighted) cartesian Hessian, \fB\-\-namespace\fP compatible.
.RE
.sp
\fBcompliance.dat\fP
.RS 4
compliance analysis from an eligible Hessian run. Lists every internal
coordinate with C\dii\u and 1/C\dii\u, followed by up to 20 strongest
off\-diagonal couplings per coordinate in descending order of magnitude.
.RE
.sp
\fBxtbtopo.mol\fP
.RS 4
topology information written in molfile format.
.RE
.sp
\fBxtbopt.xyz\fP, \fBxtbopt.coord\fP
.RS 4
optimized geometry in the same format as the input geometry.
.RE
.sp
\fBxtbhess.\fP
.RS 4
distorted geometry if an imaginary frequency was found, in the format of the
input geometry (e.g. \f(CRxtbhess.xyz\fP for an xyz input)
.RE
.sp
\fBxtbopt.log\fP
.RS 4
contains all structures obtained in the geometry optimization
with the respective energy in the comment line in a XMOL formatted
trajectory
.RE
.sp
\fBxtbsiman.log\fP, \fBxtb.trj.\fIint\fP\fP
.RS 4
trajectories from MD
.RE
.sp
\fBscoord.\fIint\fP\fP
.RS 4
coordinate dump of MD
.RE
.sp
\fBfod.cub\fP
.RS 4
FOD on a cube\-type grid
.RE
.sp
\fBspindensity.cub\fP
.RS 4
spindensity on a cube\-type grid
.RE
.sp
\fBdensity.cub\fP
.RS 4
density on a cube\-type grid
.RE
.sp
\fBmolden.input\fP
.RS 4
MOs and occupation for visualisation and sTDA\-xTB calculations
.RE
.sp
\fBpcgrad\fP
.RS 4
gradient of the point charges
.RE
.sp
\fBxtb_esp.cosmo\fP
.RS 4
ESP fake cosmo output
.RE
.sp
\fBxtb_esp_profile.dat\fP
.RS 4
ESP histogramm data
.RE
.sp
\fBvibspectrum\fP
.RS 4
Turbomole style vibrational spectrum data group
.RE
.sp
\fBg98.out\fP, \fBg98l.out\fP, \fBg98_canmode.out\fP, \fBg98_locmode.out\fP
.RS 4
g98 fake output with normal or local modes
.RE
.sp
\fB.tmpxtbmodef\fP
.RS 4
input for mode following
.RE
.sp
\fBcoordprot.0\fP
.RS 4
protonated species
.RE
.sp
\fBxtblmoinfo\fP
.RS 4
centers of the localized molecular orbitals
.RE
.sp
\fBlmocent.coord\fP
.RS 4
centers of the localized molecular orbitals
.RE
.sp
\fBtmpxx\fP
.RS 4
number of recommended modes for mode following
.RE
.sp
\fBxtb_normalmodes\fP, \fBxtb_localmodes\fP
.RS 4
binary dump for mode following
.RE
.sp
\fBxtbscan.log\fP
.RS 4
relaxed or two\-dimensional scan written by \f(CR\-\-lscan\fP, format compatible with
the upstream \f(CRrelaxed_scan\fP driver.
.RE
.sp
\fBxtbts.xyz\fP, \fBxtbts.log\fP
.RS 4
transition state and its imaginary mode written by \f(CR\-\-lts\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fBpath.xyz\fP, \fBpath_autoneb.xyz\fP, \fBpath_ts_list.xyz\fP
.RS 4
band and chain\-of\-states path written by \f(CR\-\-lts\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fBirc_forward.xyz\fP, \fBirc_backward.xyz\fP, \fBirc_path.xyz\fP, \fBirc.log\fP
.RS 4
intrinsic reaction coordinate written by \f(CR\-\-lirc\fP.
.RE
.sp
\fBlandscape_<TASK>.json\fP
.RS 4
structured result written by \f(CR\-\-ljson\fP for every native and landscape task;
the tblite\-gated \f(CR\-\-dipro\fP and \f(CR\-\-ceh\fP short circuit before the writer and
write no document.
.RE
.SS "TOUCH"
.sp
\fBxtbmdok\fP
.RS 4
generated by successful MD
.RE
.sp
\fB.sccnotconverged\fP
.RS 4
generated after failed SCC with printlevel=2
.RE
.SH "WARNINGS"
.sp
\f(CRoxtb(1)\fP can generate the two types of warnings, the first warning section
is printed immediately after the normal banner at startup, summing up the
evaluation of all input sources (commandline, xcontrol, xtbrc). To check
this warnings exclusively before running an expensive calculation a
input check is implemented via the \fB\-\-define\fP flag. Please, study this
warnings carefully!
.sp
After \f(CRoxtb(1)\fP has evaluated the all input sources it immediately enters
the production mode. Severe errors will lead to an abnormal termination
which is signalled by the printout to STDERR and a non\-zero return value
(1). All non\-fatal errors are summerized in the end of the calculation
in one block, right before the timing analysis.
.sp
To aid the user to fix the problems generating these warnings a brief
summary of each warning with its respective string representation in the
output will be shown here:
.sp
\fBANCopt failed to converge the optimization\fP
.RS 4
geometry optimization has failed to converge in the given number
optimization cycles. This is not neccessary a problem if only a
small number of cycles was given for the optimization on purpose.
All further calculations are done on the last geometry of the
optimization.
.RE
.sp
\fBHessian on incompletely optimized geometry!\fP
.RS 4
This warning will be issued twice, once before the Hessian,
calculations starts (it would otherwise take some time before
this this warning could be detected) and in the warning block
in the end. The warning will be generated if the gradient norm
on the given geometry is higher than a certain threshold.
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.sp
\fB0\fP
.RS 4
normal termination of \f(CRoxtb(1)\fP
.RE
.sp
\fB1\fP
.RS 4
failure: every fatal path goes through the environment's error handler,
which prints \f(CRabnormal termination of oxtb\fP on STDERR and exits 1. An
unknown option is a warning, not a failure (exit 0), unless \fB\-\-strict\fP is
given, which turns all warnings into errors (exit 1).
.RE
.SH "BUGS"
.sp
please report all bugs with an example input, \f(CR\-\-copy\fP dump of internal settings
and the used geometry, as well as the \f(CR\-\-verbose\fP output to xtb@thch.uni\-bonn.de
